# Water-stable Mn-based MOF nanosheet as robust visible-light-responsive photocatalyst in aqueous solution

$^1$ State Key Laboratory of Catalysis, Dalian National Laboratory for Clean Energy, the Collaborative Innovation Center of Chemistry for Energy Materials, Dalian Institute of Chemical Physics, Chinese Academy of Sciences, Dalian 116023, China;   
$^2.$ Hefei National Laboratory for Physical Sciences at the Microscale, CAS Center for Excellence in Nanoscience, School of Chemistry and Materials Science, University of Science and Technology of China, Hefei 230026, China; $^3$ State Key Laboratory of Molecular Reaction Dynamics, the Collaborative Innovation Center of Chemistry for Energy Materials, Dalian Institute of Chemical Physics, Chinese Academy of Sciences, Dalin 116023, China; 4University of Chinese Academy of Sciences, Beijing 100049, China;   
5National & Local Joint Engineering Research Center for Applied Technology of Hybrid Nanomaterials, Collaborative Innovation Center of Nano Functional Materials and Applications of Henan Province, Henan University, Kaifeng 475000, China
Development of visible-light-responsive metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) for promising solar energy conversion has inspired continuous interest, but it still suffers from poor stability especially in aqueous solution and/or under photoirradiation due to their relatively labile coordination bonds. Herein, a new synthesized layered Mn-based MOF (Mn-TBAPy-BK) and its exfoliated twodemensional nanosheets (Mn-TBAPy-NS) are revealed to be highly stable no matter in aqueous solution with wide pH region or under visible light irradiation. As expected, the exfoliated Mn-TBAPy-NS exhibits enhanced surface area, shortened charge transfer distance, and superior charge separation with respect to the bulk one. Moreover, the as-obtained nanosheets can be employed as effective building block for nanocomposite and heterostructure assembly to further promote charge separation as well as solar hydrogen production from aqueous solution under visible light. The finding of water stable MOF nanosheet under visible light demonstrates its promising future for solar energy conversion.
## metal-organic framework, nanosheet, water stable, photocatalyst, nanocomposite
Citation: Xiao Y, Guo X, Yang T, Liu J, Liu X, Xiao Y, Liu L, Liu T, Ye S, Jiang J, Zhang F, Li C. Water-stable Mn-based MOF nanosheet as robust visible-light-responsive photocatalyst in aqueous solution. Sc China Chem, 2020, 63, https://doi.org/10.1007/s11426-020-9809-x
Two-dimensional (2D) nanosheets with ultrathin thickness have achieved fast-paced development in both fundamental researches and proof-of-concept applications such as Li-ions batteries, supercapacitors, sensors and catalysis due to their exceptionally functional properties [1–5]. During the past decades, various 2D nanosheets have been exploited for promising solar energy conversion, which are composed of graphene-based materials, layered double hydroxides (LDHs), transition-metal dichalcogenides (TMDs), transition-metal oxides, graphitic carbon nitrides and so on [5–8]. Compared with the corresponding bulks, the 2D nanosheets have been demonstrated to not only undergo shortened transfer distance of photoexcited electrons and holes for promoted charge separation, but also own higher surface area to provide more active sites [6–11]. Consequently, 2D nanosheets usually exhibit remarkably enhanced catalytic performances with respect to corresponding bulks [9–11].
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs), composed of metal ions or clusters and polydentate organic linkers, are one of the most promising materials for various applications containing solar-to-chemical energy conversion due to their diversity, large surface area and structural tunability [12–17]. To date, some visible-light-responsive MOFs such as UiO-66 $\left(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\right)$ , $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -Ti-MOF, Al-PMOF and Cd-TBAPy have been demonstrated for feasible water reduction [18–22], but all of them still suffer from low activity due to their intrinsic poor charge separation efficiency [23,24]. Additionally, most of MOFs suffer from low thermal and/or photochemical water stability with effective activity observed just in the organic phase instead of aqueous solution in consideration of their relatively labile coordination bonds [16,21,25]. These two challenges greatly retard the application of MOFs-related materials for solar-to-chemical conversion such as water splitting and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction. The 2D structure of MOFs can be reasonably expected to address their poor charge separation as well as low activity, but which commonly confronts much worse chemical, thermal and/or photochemical stability in aqueous solution compared to the bulk one. Moreover, the intrinsic instability of MOFs material also impedes the preparation of MOF nanosheets through the topdown delamination which is a more effective and scalable method than the bottom-up synthesis [26,27]. To the best of our knowledge, the visible-light-responsive 2D nanosheets of MOF photocatalysts which are exfoliated from their corresponding bulks with robust chemical and photochemical stability in aqueous solution have not been reported yet.
Herein, we report a new layered manganese-organic framework (denoted as “Mn-TBAPy-BK”) with ultrahigh stability by employing 1,3,6,8-tetrakis $\dot{\boldsymbol{p}}$ -benzoic acid)pyrene $\mathrm{(H_{4}T B A P y)}$ as the organic linker (Figure S1, Supporting Information online), based on which the corresponding 2D nanosheets (denoted as “Mn-TBAPy-NS”) can be further obtained by a simple sonication exfoliated process. Strikingly, the exfoliated 2D nanosheets are highly stable in aqueous solution with and without light irradiation as well. Moreover, the exfoliated nanosheets can be used as building block for assembly of nanocomposite together with $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ nanoparticles for enhanced charge separation as well as solar hydrogen production from aqueous solution under visible light irradiation.
The as-synthesized manganese-based MOF single crystal (Mn-TBAPy-BK) with the molecular formula of $[\mathrm{Mn}_{2}.$ (TBAPy) $\left(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\right)_{2}]$ ·Dioxane $2\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ is a layered framework crystalized in monoclinic space group $P2/m$ on the basis of single-crystal $\mathrm{\DeltaX}$ -ray diffraction (XRD) analyses. As shown in Figure 1(a), each $\mathrm{Mn}^{2+}$ ion coordinates with five oxygen atoms, one $(\mathrm{O}_{1})$ from water molecule and the other four $(\mathrm{O}_{2}-$ ${\bf O}_{5})$ ) from carboxylate O atoms in different TBAPy linkers. Four TBAPy linkers bridge two symmetry-related $\mathrm{Mn}^{2+}$ centers into a classical paddle-wheel configuration. The Mn– Mn distance is about $3.06\mathrm{\AA}$ , while the band length of Mn–O is ${\sim}2.05\mathrm{\AA}$ for $\mathrm{O}_{1}$ and ${\sim}2.15\mathrm{\AA}$ for the others. Dinuclear subunits coordinate with $\mathrm{TBAPy}^{4-}$ anions to form 2D sheet (Figure 1(b)), and the 2D eclipsed structure is formed subsequently as all single layers are orderly stacked with a interlayer spacing of ${\sim}4.47\mathrm{~\AA~}$ (Figure 1(c)). There are two kinds of 1D channels along the $a$ axis whose regular window is $10.84\mathrm{~\AA}{\times}11.59\mathrm{~\AA}$ and $10.94\mathrm{~\AA}{\times}11.86\mathrm{~\AA}$ (Figure 1(d)), respectively. The total solvent-accessible volume calculated with PLATON software is approximately $25.4\%$ [28], and the Brunauer-Emmett-Teller (BET) surface area was measured to be $598\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ . The experimental powder XRD pattern (Figure S2) of Mn-TBAPy-BK matches well with the simulated one generated on the basis of single crystal structure analysis, showing a good phase purity of the bulk material. Details of the crystal structure and refinement data are provided in Table S1 (Supporting Information online). The effective coordination between manganese ions and $\mathrm{H_{4}T B A P y}$ linkers can also be confirmed by the disappearance of hydroxyl vibration peak of –COOH in the range of $3,000{-}3,400\ \mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ and the shift of carbonyl vibration peak in the range of $1,620{-}1,750\ \mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ during the Fourier transform infrared (FT-IR) measurements (Figure S3).
The as-obtained layered manganese-organic framework exhibits excellent chemical stability no matter by calcining it in nitrogen at below $723\mathrm{~K~}$ (Figure S4), or by soaking it in various solvents or in aqueous solutions with $\mathsf{p H}$ value of 2–11 for $12\mathrm{h}$ at room temperature (Figure S5). By a simple ultrasound-assisted liquid exfoliation process, the MnTBAPy-NS nanosheets with regular rectangle morphology (Figure 2(a)) and average thickness of $9.5\mathrm{nm}$ (Figure 2(b)) can be obtained. It is interesting to note that the Mn-TBAPyNS inherits crystallinity from Mn-TBAPy-BK (Figures S6 and S2), and the average pore sizes of Mn-TBAPy-BK and Mn-TBAPy-NS samples were also measured to be similar (4.825 and $4.875\mathrm{~\AA~}$ , respectively, Figure S7). The element analyses of Mn-TBAPy-BK and Mn-TBAPy-NS were also conducted and both of them are similar to the theoretical values (Table S2), indicating the good stability of MnTBAPy-NS. Additionally, as given in the ultraviolet-visible (UV-Vis) spectra (Figure 2(c)), the Mn-TBAPy-NS and MnTBAPy-BK samples exhibit similar band-to-band transition with absorption edge of ${\sim}500~\mathrm{nm}$ , demonstrating their good feature of semiconductor. It should be pointed out that the slight red shift $(\sim16\mathrm{nm})$ of absorption edge on Mn-TBAPyNS may be attributed to the enhanced intralayer conjugation caused by the reduced interlayer repulsion force during the exfoliation process [29]. All of above results demonstrate that the exfoliated nanosheet can well maintain the basic structure of the bulk MOF to exhibit good chemical stability. Moreover, it is worth noting that compared to the corresponding bulk sample, the exfoliated Mn-TBAPy-NS nanosheet exhibits significantly enhanced surface area (4,565 vs. $598\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g})$ ), remarkably decreased charge transfer resistance (Figure 2(d) and Table S3), and much weakened photoluminescence (PL) peak intensity (Figure 2(e)). It means that the exfoliated nanosheet not only provides much more active sites, but also favors the charge separation of photogenerated carriers.
Besides these merits stemmed from the nanosheet structure, the exfoliated Mn-TBAPy-NS with 2D nanostructure can also be reasonably expected as building block for assembly of nanocomposite to further promote spatial charge separation of MOF semiconductor. For this purpose, the thermodynamical position of conduction bands (CB) of the Mn-TBAPy-NS sample was first evaluated by Mott-Schottky (M-S) measurements to be $-0.40\ \mathrm{eV}$ (Figure S8(a)) [30]. Together with the results of UV-Vis spectra (Figure 2(c)) and X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) analysis (Figure S8 (b)), its valence band (VB) is deduced to be located at $2.08\mathrm{eV.}$ It is worth noting that the band edge positions of Mn-TBAPy-NS are well satisfied with the thermodynamic requirement for reduction of $_{\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}}$ (or $\mathrm{CO}_{2}\cdot$ ) and water oxidation respectively. Similarly, the CB and VB positions of typical $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ semiconductors were evaluated with results given in Figure S9. According to above analysis results, the relative band edge positions between the exfoliated 2D nanosheet and $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ can thermodynamically drive the spatial separation of photogenerated electrons, which are given in Figure 2(f). As a blueprint illustration, the nanocomposite of Mn-TBAPy- ${\bf\cdot N S}/\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ can be simply prepared by the selfaggregation method. By adjusting the pH environment which is located between the isoelectric point (IEP) of Mn-TBAPyNS (IEP: 2.1) and titania (IEP: 6.4, Figure S10), the electrostatic attraction force derived from the opposite surface charges will drive the successful self-assembly of MnTBAPy- ${\bf\cdot N S}/\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ nanocomposite (Figures S11 and S12). In this case, the promoted spatial charge separation can be really achieved according to the observation of decreased PL intensity and lifetime on the composite with respect to the single Mn-TBAPy-NS sample (Figure 2(e) and Figure S13).
Encouraged by its good chemical stability in aqueous solution and suitable band positions, the potential of MnTBAPy-NS for solar-to-chemical conversion was evaluated by the probe reaction of photocatalytic water splitting with platinum photodeposited as cocatalyst to provide active sites. As expected, no obvious hydrogen evolution can be detected without light irradiation and/or free of the Mn-TBAPy-NS sample. However, the platinum modified Mn-TBAPy-NS photocatalyst can obviously drive the water splitting to steadily evolve hydrogen in the aqueous solution by using different hole scanvengers (Figure S14), which demonstrates that the photocatalytic $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ evolution of Mn-TBAPy-NS from aqueous solution is of universality. As a comparison, the exfoliated Mn-TBAPy-NS photocatalyst exhibit more superior $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ evolution rate under visible light irradiation with respect to the corresponding bulk Mn-TBAPy-BK photocatalyst (Figure 3(a), about 11 times), as should result from above observations of enhanced surface area and promoted charge separation on the Mn-TBAPy-NS sample (Figure 2(d, e)). Interestingly, the $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ evolution rate on the Mn-TBAPyNS sample can be further promoted by about 16 times on the Mn-TBAPy- ${\bf\cdot N S}/\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ nanocomposite with optimized composition (Figure 3(a) and Figure S15), demonstrating the feasibility of assembly of the nanosheets in promoting the charge separation process (Figure 2(f) and Figure S13) as well as the photocatalytic performance. The wavelength dependent photocatalytic reactions of Mn-TBAPy-NS and Mn-TBAPy- ${\bf\cdot N S}/\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ were also carried out, and the strong dependence of $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ evolution rate on the wavelength of the irradiation light indicates that the $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ evolution is driven by the visible light (Figure S16). Moreover, the nanosheet-based composite photocatalyst also exhibits good photochemical stability in the experimental region according to the multiple cycles of reaction results (Figure 3(b)). In order to further illustrate the advantage functionality of Mn-TBAPy-NS nanosheets as the building block, we further make a coarse assembly of heterostructure composed of Mn-TBAPy-NS nanosheets and visible-light-responsive TaON photocatalyst, whose conduction and valance bands were separately evaluated and demonstrated to be satisfied with requirement of the type II heterostructure (Figure S17). As shown in Figure S17(d), the obvious promotion of photocatalytic $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ evolution rate on the Mn-TBAPy-NS photocatalyst can be obtained, further revealing the feasibility of the Mn-TBAPy-NS nanosheet as building block. All of these results reveal the bright future of the novel Mn-TBAPy-NS as nanosheetbased photocatalysts for promising solar-to-chemical conversion.
To get insight into the good stability of the novel material in aqueous solution, the effect of water molecules on the structure of the Mn-based MOF in this work and our early reported Cd-based MOF with the same linker $\mathrm{(H_{4}T B A P y)}$ but only stable in organic phase [21] were theoretically simulated for comparison. Figure 4 gives the theoretically optimized model structures of the Mn-TBAPy-BK (010) and Cd-TBAPy (010) with and without water adsorption, respectively. It is easy to see that the Mn-TBAPy-BK MOF can retain its original structure after adsorption of $_\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ , while the $\mathrm{Cd\mathrm{-}O_{1}}$ bond in Cd-TBAPy would like to be broken due to the effect of adsorbed water to finally cause destroy of the MOF framework. The adsorption energy $(E_{\mathrm{ads}})$ of $_{\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}}$ on the Mnbased MOF $(1.22\mathrm{eV})$ is much bigger than that of Cd-based MOF $(0.58\mathrm{eV})$ , as listed in Table S4. A larger positive value of $E_{\mathrm{ads}}$ indicates an exothermic sorption process and a more stable MOF system, which is responsible for the fact that the Mn-based MOF in this work is stable in water, while the Cdbased MOF can be only stable in the organic phase. Additionally, the better water stability of Mn-based MOF with respect to the Cd-based MOF can also be explained according to the Pearson’s hard-soft-acid-base (HSAB) principle [16], where much stronger coordination bonds (Table S5) are formed between the manganese ions as hard acid and carboxylate-based ligands $\mathrm{(H_{4}T B A P y)}$ as hard base.
Development of new visible-light-responsive photocatalyst for solar energy conversion is of great significance on account of environmental and energy issues [31–35], and the exploitation of inorganic-organic hybrid MOFs photocatalysts has recently inspired extensive interest in consideration of their special advantages in the design and adjustment of structure and functionality [12–17]. However, MOFs-related materials always suffer from their intrinsic poor charge separation, bad chemical and/or photochemical stability especially in aqueous solution in consideration of their relatively labile coordination bonds [16,23–25]. Inspired by inorganic 2D photocatalysts, the nanosheet structure of MOF can be reasonably expected to exhibit superior charge separation efficiency as the shortened charge transfer distance and enhanced surface active sites exposed [5–11]. However, the robust MOF nanosheet is more difficultly prepared because of its even weaker coordination bonds with respect to the bulk one [26,27,36]. To date, the water stable MOF nanosheet photocatalyst with visible-light response which is exfoliated from its corresponding bulk has not been reported yet. In this work, we give the first report on preparation of Mn-based MOF nanosheet that is highly stable in aqueous solution with wide pH region and under visible light irradiation. As a proof-of-concept illustration, we reveal that compared to the bulk one, the Mn-TBAPy-NS nanosheet not only exhibits basic structural advantages in enhancing surface area (from 598 to $4{,}565\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g})$ and shortening charge transfer distance to cause better charge separation (Figure 2 (d, e)), but also shows special application in assembling nanocomposite together with titania to further promote charge separation (Figure 2(e), Figure S13) as well as photocatalytic performance (Figure 3). Based on the preparation of MOF nanosheet and its assembly, the $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ evolution rate on the new synthesized Mn-TBAPy-BK photocatalyst can be promoted by about 11 and 176 times, respectively. As a blueprint, the 2D nanostructure of MOF nanosheet can be reasonably expected as effective building block for heterostructure assembly as well, which is under investigation. Our work demonstrates the importance and bright future in exploiting nanosheet MOF photocatalyst for promising solarto-chemcial conversion.
